data.content:
Your code **doesn't use `Thread.Abort`**, `Thread.Interrupt`, or catch `ThreadAbortException`. It only uses `Thread.Start()`, `Thread.Join()`, and `Thread.Sleep()` — all of which work identically in .NET 8 with no migration needed.

**That said**, the code would benefit from modernization using `Task` and `CancellationToken` for better resource usage and cooperative cancellation support:

```csharp
public class SimpleWorker
{
    public async Task RunBatchAsync(List<WorkItem> items, CancellationToken ct = default)
    {
        var tasks = items.Select(item => Task.Run(() => Process(item, ct), ct));
        await Task.WhenAll(tasks);
    }

    private void Process(WorkItem item, CancellationToken ct)
    {
        ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
        Thread.Sleep(100); // or: await Task.Delay(100, ct) if async
        item.Result = ComputeResult(item.Input);
    }
}
```

This gives you cancellation support without `Thread.Abort`, but your original code will compile and run fine on .NET 8 as-is.
